  "narrative": "<h3 class=\"story-subheading\">Short impact summary</h3>\n<p class=\"story-text\">Comics of the New Europe shows early signs of real use and reach: it has been downloaded 2,249 times, appears to have informed two recorded discussions, and was supported through Knowledge Unlatched. The record is still modest on formal citation impact, with zero OpenAlex citations so far, so the strongest evidence today is readership and teaching-facing visibility rather than scholarly citation uptake.</p>\n<h3 class=\"story-subheading\">Full narrative</h3>\n<p class=\"story-text\">Comics of the New Europe is a 2023 scholarly volume from Leuven University Press that brings comics studies into conversation with the political and cultural histories of post\u2013Iron Curtain Europe. Its conceptual profile centers on comics, memoir, communism, history, and narrative, reflecting the book\u2019s focus on graphic narratives from countries such as East Germany, Poland, the Czech Republic, Hungary, Romania, Yugoslavia, and Ukraine. The volume is openly available in a hybrid model, which helps explain its visible usage.</p>\n<p class=\"story-text\">The clearest impact signal in the record is sustained readership. OAPEN reports 2,249 downloads, indicating active use beyond an initial publication burst. That pattern suggests the book is being sought out by readers with interest in comics scholarship, cultural history, and area studies, although download data should be read as evidence of access and attention rather than citation-based scholarly influence.</p>\n<p class=\"story-text\">There is also some indication of teaching or educational uptake. The book is discussed in two recorded YouTube videos, one from NYU\u2019s Jordan Center and one from the Czech Literary Centre. These references do not establish formal syllabus adoption, but they do show that the volume has entered public scholarly and cultural discussion in ways that can support teaching, outreach, and specialist conversation.</p>\n<p class=\"story-text\">The citation record is still early. OpenAlex currently shows no citations, so there is not yet a citation-based signal of academic accumulation. In a book of this kind, that is not unusual soon after publication; the available evidence instead points to circulation, discoverability, and use. Funding support from Knowledge Unlatched further aligns the book with open-access dissemination, which likely contributes to its download performance.</p>\n<p class=\"story-text\">Overall, the record portrays a book with clear readership and some educational visibility, but limited formal scholarly citation uptake so far. Its strongest impact lies in making an underexamined area of comics scholarship more accessible and usable across research and teaching contexts.</p>",
